From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xob0t <32616886+xob0t@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 05:46:59 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/22] Inlined constructors: temporaries of the constructor
 written out; constructor calls IL does not accept are marked with a note

- ConstructorFolding compares the statements after a raw allocation with the
  constructor's. A constructor that keeps a value computed from its parameters in a
  temporary ("double norm = offset / 255; this.offsetNorm = norm;") never matched a
  site where the compiler knew the arguments: there the value is a constant stored
  straight into the field. The constructor's statements are now also compared with
  such temporaries written where they are read (REWIND_NO_CONSTRUCTOR_TEMP_PROPAGATION
  turns this off); the existing argument solving then finds the constant.
- Where the class's own constructor still is not put back but a base class's
  constructor was, as a call on the raw object, the code does what the original did
  but is no IL a verifier accepts (CallCtor). The method is now reported incomplete
  and the place gets a marker that does not stop the code: a __Rewind.Note at the
  allocation (HOpaque.Remark, written as Note instead of Effect;
  REWIND_NO_MARKED_RAW_CONSTRUCTORS turns this off).
- The same kind of marker now stands where BaseConstructorCalls finds no base
  constructor call to put back (it was an Effect, which made such a constructor throw
  where it had run as before).

Unity 6000.0 Master x64 game, Assembly-CSharp: raw allocations 286 -> 212 (74 put
back as "new"); CallCtor in methods without a marker 46 -> 2; 36 methods now carry
the note.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
